WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
DAVID MALAN: Hello, world.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.320
My name is David Malan, and this is
CS50, Harvard University's introduction

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.660
to the intellectual
enterprises of computer science

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.450
and the art of programming for
majors and non-majors alike.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.110
Indeed, whether you're among
those less comfortable or more

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:18.110
comfortable with technology, this
course, and computer science more

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.310
generally, are in fact for you.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.470
And it is free via platforms like
edX, YouTube, Apple TV, Google TV,

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.640
and CS50's own website.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.930
Through this course, you'll learn
how to think more methodically, more

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.650
algorithmically, so to speak.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.680
You'll learn how to
communicate succinctly

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.980
and precisely, and you'll learn how to
solve problems efficiently with code.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.250
Indeed, this course begins
at the very beginning

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.770
with a very friendly graphical
programming language called Scratch,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.100
via which you'll write code
by dragging and dropping

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.140
puzzle pieces that
only interlock together

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.250
if it makes logical sense to do so.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.970
We'll then transition to C, a more
traditional keyboard-based language,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.630
via which you'll learn how computers
work underneath the hood, so to speak.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.670
Thereafter, the course
moves on to Python,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.350
a more modern language via which you can
analyze data, automate processes, build

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.190
web applications, and more, and SQL,
via which you can read and write

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.900
lots and lots of data in databases.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.670
Toward course's end, we'll dive
into HTML, CSS, and JavaScript,

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.920
languages with which you can create
web apps and mobile apps alike.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.320
And at course's end, you'll design and
implement your very own final project

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.830
for the world to see.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.230
You will have learned how to program.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.410
And if at any point
you get stuck, there's

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:32.210
a whole CS50 community to support you,
as well as nowadays a virtual rubber

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:36.840
duck of whom you can also ask
questions anytime, thanks to AI.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.500
All this and more, this is CS50.

